  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nPleasant conditions in place across the region today with surface \nhigh pressure overhead and drier air with dewpoints in the 50s. \nOvernight, temperatures look to fall into the 50s for most locations \nwith increasing high clouds in advance of an approaching mid-level \nshortwave. This wave will come up from the southwest and is still \ntaking aim at the central/northern plains Tuesday into Tuesday \nevening. Latest timing from the models would bring showers into the \nsouthwest CWA late Tuesday morning or closer to 18Z, with activity \nthen spreading northeast across portions of the remaining CWA \nthrough the afternoon and into the evening hours. Severe storm \npotential looks limited over the region, with the better instability \nsetting up further south across southern SD and into NE/IA closer to \nthe surface boundary. That said, HREF ensemble MUCAPE values do \nreach 750-1000 J/KG by 00Z Wed over Jones/Lyman counties. Newest Day \n2 SPC Convective Outlook does bring the Marginal Risk area right up \nto the bootheel of Lyman County. As for rainfall amounts, NBM mean \nQPF values are generally from 0.25in to 0.60in across the region, \nbut any areas of heavier rainfall in thunderstorms could bring \nupwards of an inch or more as noted by NBM90th amounts between 1.00-\n1.50in. \n\nLooking further ahead towards the end of the week and upcoming \nweekend, it still appears the overall upper pattern will feature a \nlarge building ridge across the central CONUS, poking northward into \nthe Northern Plains. Grand Ensemble mean 850mb temps Thursday \nthrough the weekend generally show +24C to +27C across the region, \nwhich is running around the 95th to 98th percentiles for this time \nof year. High temperatures Thursday through Saturday are forecast in \nthe upper 80s to mid 90s across the CWA, warmest over central SD. \nDewpoints will be creeping upward as well, with inherited NBM \nreadings in the mid/upper 60s to around 70 degrees across the \neastern CWA.",
